  "full_response": "Certainly! Below are examples of `curl` commands for authenticating with various APIs using different authentication methods: Bearer token, API key, and Basic Authentication.\n\n### 1. Bearer Token Authentication\nTo authenticate using a Bearer token, you typically include the token in the `Authorization` header.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \"https://api.example.com/endpoint\" \\\n     -H \"Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\"\n```\n\n### 2. API Key Authentication\nFor API key authentication, you generally add the key in a specific header, often named `X-API-Key`.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \"https://api.example.com/endpoint\" \\\n     -H \"X-API-Key: ak_live_1234567890\"\n```\n\n### 3. Basic Authentication\nBasic authentication requires the username and password to be encoded and included in the `Authorization` header, typically using Base64 encoding.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X GET \"https://api.example.com/endpoint\" \\\n     -u \"admin:password123\"\n```\n\nAlternatively, you can manually encode the credentials using base64 encoding and include them in the header:\n\n```bash\n# Encode credentials\necho -n \"admin:password123\" | base64\n\n# Include in Authorization header (let's say the encoded result is YWRtaW46cGFzc3dvcmQxMjM0)\ncurl -X GET \"https://api.example.com/endpoint\" \\\n     -H \"Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46cGFzc3dvcmQxMjM0\"\n```\n\nThese examples demonstrate how you can use `curl` to authenticate with APIs using different methods. Always ensure that your authentication credentials are kept secure and not exposed in your scripts or version control.",
